NeuroFabric OS: A Formal Architecture for an AI-Native Real-Time Operating System for General-Purpose Humanoid Robots**


ouadi Maakoul 

AI: Claude, chatGpt, Deepseek 

 Abstract

The emergence of general-purpose humanoid robots capable of operating in unstructured human environments demands a fundamental reconceptualization of operating system design. Classical paradigms — designed for deterministic, single-host, von-Neumann architectures — are architecturally insufficient for a cyber-physical organism that must simultaneously execute sub-millisecond reflex responses, sub-second motor planning, and multi-second cognitive reasoning, while maintaining sensorimotor coherence across heterogeneous compute fabrics (CPU, GPU, NPU, MCU). This dissertation introduces *NeuroFabric OS*, a formally specified five-layer hybrid operating system architecture designed natively for general-purpose humanoid robots.

We present a complete mathematical formalization of the system as a *Hierarchical Temporal Compute Graph* (HTCG) over directed acyclic graphs with temporal decorations, and derive end-to-end latency bounds by structural induction. We provide the full Worst-Case Response Time (WCRT) analysis for the reflex scheduler, proving schedulability via both Rate-Monotonic utilization bounds and exact iterative response-time equations, and formally prove the Critical Instant Theorem upon which both analyses rest. We formulate the Whole-Body Control quadratic program with its complete KKT optimality conditions, prove strong duality under Slater's constraint qualification, and characterize real-time feasibility via active-set complexity analysis. We derive the DDPM forward and reverse diffusion processes from first principles using the variational Evidence Lower Bound, and integrate the resulting policy into the Neural Module abstraction with a formal parameter-update safety bound. We introduce a *Distributed Embodied Memory* (DEM) hierarchy with a stochastic retention policy grounded in the exponential forgetting model, and a formal episodic-to-semantic consolidation operator. The *simulation-native execution* framework is grounded in Invariant Extended Kalman Filtering on the Lie group $SE_2(3)$, with formal divergence detection via Mahalanobis distance. The NP-hardness of HTCG scheduling is established via tight polynomial reduction from 3-PARTITION. We prove a *Cognitive Fault Isolation* theorem guaranteeing postural safety under arbitrary cognitive-layer failure. Together, these contributions constitute a rigorous theoretical foundation for a new class of AI-native operating system.

**Keywords:** humanoid robotics, real-time operating systems, rate-monotonic scheduling, whole-body control, diffusion policy, embodied AI, GPU DAG scheduling, Kalman filtering, Lie groups, cyber-physical systems.

