## Chapter 1 — Introduction

### 1.1 Motivation

The general-purpose humanoid robot represents the most complex cyber-physical system ever engineered. It is simultaneously a mechanical system governed by rigid-body dynamics, a sensory organism processing multi-modal streams at high frequency, a cognitive agent capable of natural language interaction and abstract reasoning, and an adaptive entity that must accumulate skill knowledge across operational sessions without catastrophic forgetting.

Despite converging advances in large behaviour models, whole-body control theory, compact neuromorphic processors, and high-energy-density batteries, the software infrastructure beneath these systems remains architecturally immature. A modular and hierarchical structure is well recognised as appropriate for humanoid software, yet many models suitable for different tasks — visual understanding, motion planning, and low-level control — are currently isolated and have not yet been successfully integrated into a coherent runtime framework. Many models suitable for different tasks on humanoid robots — visual understanding, motion planning, control — are often isolated and have not yet been successfully integrated and implemented.

This dissertation addresses precisely this integration gap. The argument is not that individual components are insufficient, but that a *unified operating system abstraction* is missing: one that coordinates the full stack — from motor torque loops to language reasoning — under a coherent set of formal guarantees.

### 1.2 The Core Thesis

**Thesis Statement.** A general-purpose humanoid robot cannot be adequately served by any existing operating system paradigm. It requires a novel *AI-native, GPU-primary, graph-orchestrated, simulation-native* operating system architecture that treats neural policies as first-class system objects, separates reflex computation from cognitive computation with formal timing contracts, supports exact real-time schedulability analysis for heterogeneous neural compute graphs, and maintains a hierarchically structured embodied memory that persists across operational sessions.

### 1.3 Contributions

This dissertation makes the following original contributions:

**C1.** Formal definition of the *Hierarchical Temporal Compute Graph* (HTCG) as the unifying mathematical object for humanoid runtime orchestration, together with a complete structural-induction proof of its end-to-end latency bound.

**C2.** A five-layer architecture for NeuroFabric OS with formal interface contracts between layers and a complete proof of the *Cognitive Fault Isolation Theorem*.

**C3.** A complete schedulability analysis for the reflex layer including the Critical Instant Theorem, Rate-Monotonic utilization bound, and exact Worst-Case Response Time (WCRT) iterative fixed-point analysis.

**C4.** A tight polynomial reduction proving NP-hardness of the HTCG heterogeneous scheduling problem via 3-PARTITION.

**C5.** A full mathematical treatment of the Whole-Body Control quadratic program including KKT optimality conditions, Slater's constraint qualification, and real-time solve complexity via sparse Cholesky.

**C6.** A rigorous derivation of the DDPM diffusion policy from the Evidence Lower Bound, including complete noise schedule mathematics and score-matching equivalence.

**C7.** The *Distributed Embodied Memory* hierarchy with formal stochastic retention policy and episodic-to-semantic consolidation operator.

**C8.** An Invariant Extended Kalman Filter (InEKF) formulation on $SE_2(3)$ for digital twin synchronization, with formal Mahalanobis divergence detection guarantees.

### 1.4 Scope and Limitations

This dissertation is theoretical. We propose formal architectures and derive mathematical properties, but do not implement the full system on physical hardware. Validation is performed through formal analysis, references to supporting empirical literature, and computational illustrations where noted. All WCET figures cited in scheduling examples are representative estimates derived from literature benchmarks, not measured values.

