## Chapter 2 — Background and Related Work

### 2.1 Classical Operating Systems and Their Inadequacy

Classical operating systems were designed around a model of computation fundamentally different from the requirements of an embodied robot. A Linux process operates on a flat address space, communicates via IPC primitives, and is scheduled by a general-purpose scheduler optimized for throughput rather than temporal guarantees. The real-time extension `PREEMPT_RT` linearizes the kernel lock hierarchy and converts most interrupt handlers to preemptible kernel threads, yielding deterministic worst-case latency on a single processor. However, it does not address the challenges of heterogeneous compute fabrics, GPU-native neural policy execution, or sensorimotor coherence across sub-systems with radically different timing requirements.

### 2.2 ROS 2 and Its Structural Limitations

The Robot Operating System 2 (ROS 2) represents the dominant middleware in current robotics research. It provides publish-subscribe communication over the Data Distribution Service (DDS) transport, a node-based computation model, and a lifecycle state machine. For a general-purpose humanoid, ROS 2 presents four structural limitations. First, the DDS layer introduces non-deterministic serialization latency in the median case of tens to hundreds of microseconds for large tensor payloads from neural networks. Second, ROS 2 was designed for CPU-resident computation and provides no primitives for GPU task graphs or CUDA memory management. Third, the ROS 2 executor does not provide formal real-time guarantees: callback dispatch is not priority-preemptive and the executor can starve high-priority callbacks under load. Fourth, the ROS 2 topic model uses serialized message copying that is architecturally incompatible with zero-copy GPU shared memory.

### 2.3 Whole-Body Control: State of the Art

Whole-body control (WBC) in humanoid robots formulates locomotion as a prioritised constrained optimisation problem over joint torques and contact forces, treating the entire robot as a single kinematically and dynamically coupled system rather than decomposing it into independent subsystems. A whole-body dynamic walking controller implemented as a convex quadratic program can solve an optimal control problem using an approximate value function derived from a simple walking model while respecting the dynamic, input, and contact constraints of the full robot dynamics. By exploiting sparsity and temporal structure in the optimisation with a custom active-set algorithm, control rates of 1 kHz are achievable for a 34-DOF humanoid.

Hierarchical quadratic programming (HQP) is commonly applied to consider strict hierarchies of multi-tasks and robot physical inequality constraints during whole-body compliance.

### 2.4 Diffusion Policies for Robot Control

Diffusion Policy learns the gradient of the action-distribution score function and iteratively optimises with respect to this gradient field during inference via a series of stochastic Langevin dynamics steps. The diffusion formulation yields powerful advantages including gracefully handling multimodal action distributions, being suitable for high-dimensional action spaces, and exhibiting impressive training stability.

### 2.5 State Estimation on Lie Groups

Contact-aided invariant extended Kalman filtering defines the error dynamics on a matrix Lie group, yielding log-linear error equations and superior convergence and consistency properties compared to quaternion-based extended Kalman filters. For humanoid base-state estimation, the Right Invariant Extended Kalman Filter (RIEKF) with state representation on $SE_4(3)$ achieves reduced drift in localisation and faster estimation convergence.

### 2.6 GPU DAG Scheduling

A GPU runtime scheduler for multi-task computations can transparently provide asynchronous execution, space-sharing, and transfer-computation overlap by treating the workload as a directed acyclic graph, achieving substantial speedups against synchronous execution. Reinforcement learning models frame GPU scheduling as a Markov Decision Process where states encode resource utilisation and queue lengths, actions correspond to dispatch decisions, and a Graph Neural Network captures DAG-structured workloads to learn execution orderings that minimise end-to-end runtime.

### 2.7 Summary of the Gap

No existing system unifies whole-body control, neural policy execution, GPU-native scheduling, embodied memory, and cognitive reasoning under a single architectural framework with formal guarantees. NeuroFabric OS fills this gap.

