## Chapter 3 — Problem Formulation and System Model

### 3.1 The Humanoid as a Cyber-Physical Organism

We model a general-purpose humanoid robot as a *cyber-physical organism* $\mathcal{H}$:

$$\mathcal{H} = (\mathcal{B}, \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{C}, \mathcal{E})$$

where $\mathcal{B}$ is the mechanical body: a rigid-body kinematic tree of $n_b$ links connected by $m$ actuated joints, with configuration space $\mathcal{Q} = SE(3) \times \mathbb{R}^m$ (floating-base plus joint angles); $\mathcal{S}$ is the sensor set comprising cameras (RGB, depth), inertial measurement units (IMU), force-torque sensors, microphones, and tactile arrays; $\mathcal{A}$ is the actuator set of torque-controlled joints and end-effectors; $\mathcal{C}$ is the heterogeneous compute fabric $\{CPU, GPU, NPU, MCU_1, \ldots, MCU_k\}$; and $\mathcal{E}$ is an open, partially observable, dynamic physical world.

### 3.2 Generalized Coordinates and the Floating-Base Convention

Let $q \in \mathcal{Q}$ denote the generalized coordinate vector. Under the floating-base convention, we decompose:

$$q = \begin{bmatrix} q_{fb} \\ q_j \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{6+m}$$

where $q_{fb} = (p_{base}, \Phi_{base}) \in \mathbb{R}^3 \times SO(3)$ encodes the six-dimensional pose of the base link in the world frame (represented in minimal Euler or unit-quaternion form depending on context), and $q_j \in \mathbb{R}^m$ are the actuated joint angles. The generalised velocity is $\dot{q} = [\nu_{fb}^T, \dot{q}_j^T]^T$ where $\nu_{fb} = (v_{base}, \omega_{base}) \in \mathbb{R}^6$ is the spatial velocity of the base.

### 3.3 The Fundamental Multi-Timescale Problem

The central challenge of humanoid operating system design is the *multi-timescale coherence problem*. The system must simultaneously satisfy computations across radically different temporal regimes:

| Timescale | Function | Target Frequency | Compute Target |
|---|---|---|---|
| $\tau_{\mu}$ | Motor torque, balance reflex | 1 kHz | MCU |
| $\tau_{c}$ | Whole-body controller (WBC) | 200–500 Hz | CPU/GPU |
| $\tau_{p}$ | Motion planning (MPC) | 10–50 Hz | GPU |
| $\tau_{v}$ | Perception, sensor fusion | 30–100 Hz | GPU |
| $\tau_{r}$ | Reasoning, language | 1–5 Hz | GPU |
| $\tau_{m}$ | Memory consolidation | 0.1–1 Hz | GPU/CPU |

**Definition 3.1 (Temporal Coherence).** A humanoid operating system maintains *temporal coherence* if and only if for every pair of layers $(L_i, L_j)$ with respective nominal periods $T_i < T_j$, (i) the output of $L_j$ is consistent with the physical state observable at the time of its last completed execution, and (ii) $L_i$ does not require output from $L_j$ within any deadline shorter than $T_j$. Formally:

$$\forall (L_i, L_j) \text{ with } T_i < T_j: \quad \mathcal{D}_{i \leftarrow j} \geq T_j$$

where $\mathcal{D}_{i \leftarrow j}$ is the minimum inter-layer deadline from $L_j$ to $L_i$.

This condition formalizes the requirement that fast reflex loops cannot *block* on slow cognitive outputs, and that cognitive outputs remain physically grounded when they arrive.

### 3.4 Requirements Specification

The formal requirements $\mathcal{R}$ for NeuroFabric OS are:

**R1 (Hard Real-Time Reflexes).** The reflex layer must satisfy hard real-time constraints: worst-case response time (WCRT) $\leq 1$ ms for balance and joint torque control.

**R2 (Cognitive Independence).** Failure or indefinite suspension of any layer $L_i$ with $i \geq 3$ must not cause constraint violations in $L_1$ or $L_2$.

**R3 (Neural Policy as System Object).** Neural policies are first-class system objects with formal lifecycle semantics: typed input/output contracts, versioned parameters, GPU-sandboxed execution, hot-reload with output-distribution safety bound, and session-persistent storage.

**R4 (GPU Primacy).** All perception, planning, and policy inference must execute natively on GPU without CPU-side serialization overhead exceeding $\epsilon_{PCIe} = 10\ \mu\text{s}$.

**R5 (Embodied Persistence).** The system maintains a multi-level memory hierarchy that persists across operational sessions with formal retention policy.

**R6 (Simulation Parity).** Every system component must have a simulation-equivalent instantiation, and the physical state must remain synchronised with a digital twin within Mahalanobis distance $\delta_{sync}$.

**R7 (Graceful Degradation).** The system degrades through defined safety levels under single-component failures, maintaining minimum-safe behaviour at each level.

