## Chapter 6 — Real-Time Scheduling Theory: Complete Analysis

### 6.1 Task Model

We adopt the standard Liu-Layland periodic task model. A set of $n$ independent, preemptive, periodic tasks $\mathcal{T} = \{\tau_1, \ldots, \tau_n\}$ is characterised by $\tau_i = (C_i, T_i, D_i)$ where $C_i$ is the Worst-Case Execution Time (WCET), $T_i$ is the period, and $D_i$ is the relative deadline. We distinguish two regimes: *implicit deadlines* where $D_i = T_i$ (the Liu-Layland assumption), and *constrained deadlines* where $D_i \leq T_i$.

### 6.2 The Critical Instant Theorem

Before deriving schedulability conditions, we establish the foundational theorem.

**Definition 6.1 (Critical Instant).** A *critical instant* for task $\tau_i$ is a moment at which the response time of $\tau_i$ is maximised.

**Theorem 6.1 (Critical Instant — Liu and Layland, 1973).** Under preemptive fixed-priority scheduling with implicit deadlines, a critical instant for any task $\tau_i$ occurs when $\tau_i$ is requested simultaneously with requests for all higher-priority tasks.

*Proof.* Let $\tau_i$ have priority rank $r$ (lower rank = higher priority). Consider a job of $\tau_i$ released at time $t_0$. The response time of this job is maximised when the total interference from all higher-priority tasks $\{\tau_1, \ldots, \tau_{r-1}\}$ during the interval $[t_0, t_0 + R_i]$ is maximised, where $R_i$ is the response time.

The interference from $\tau_j$ ($j < r$, i.e., higher priority) during interval $[t_0, t_0 + R_i]$ is:

$$I_j(R_i) = \left\lceil \frac{R_i}{T_j} \right\rceil C_j$$

This quantity is non-decreasing in $R_i$ and is maximised when all jobs of $\tau_j$ are released as close as possible to $t_0$. The worst case occurs when all higher-priority tasks have their phase aligned to $t_0$, i.e., all release simultaneously at $t_0$. If the release of some higher-priority task $\tau_j$ is advanced toward $t_0$, the completion of $\tau_i$ can only be delayed or unchanged: advancing a release can never reduce preemption. Repeating this argument for all $\tau_j$ with $j < r$ establishes that simultaneous release at $t_0$ is the worst-case scenario. $\square$

### 6.3 Rate-Monotonic Scheduling: The Utilization Bound

**Definition 6.2 (Rate-Monotonic Assignment).** Under Rate-Monotonic Scheduling (RMS), task priorities are assigned inversely proportional to periods: $T_i < T_j \Rightarrow \text{priority}(\tau_i) > \text{priority}(\tau_j)$.

RMS is proven to be an optimal static-priority scheduling policy: if a set of tasks can be scheduled by any static-priority algorithm, it can also be scheduled by RMS. [Innovation](https://innovation.world/invention/rate-monotonic-scheduling/)

**Theorem 6.2 (RMS Optimality — Liu and Layland, 1973).** If a feasible priority assignment exists for some task set, the Rate-Monotonic priority assignment is feasible for that task set. [Radboud University](https://www.cs.ru.nl/~hooman/DES/liu-layland.pdf)

*Proof Sketch.* Given any feasible fixed-priority assignment, consider two tasks $\tau_i$, $\tau_j$ with adjacent priorities where $T_i > T_j$ but $\tau_i$ has higher priority. We show that swapping their priorities preserves feasibility. In the original assignment, $\tau_j$ is preempted by $\tau_i$ whenever $\tau_i$ is ready. After the swap, $\tau_j$ preempts $\tau_i$. Since $T_j < T_i$, the additional preemption load placed on $\tau_i$ by $\tau_j$ is bounded by $\lceil T_i / T_j \rceil C_j$, which is no worse than the load previously imposed by $\tau_i$ on $\tau_j$ in the original assignment. A careful case analysis on the critical instant (Theorem 6.1) shows $\tau_i$'s deadline is still met post-swap. Since any feasible fixed-priority assignment can be transformed into the rate-monotonic assignment via a finite sequence of such pairwise swaps, all of which preserve feasibility, the rate-monotonic assignment is optimal. $\square$

**Theorem 6.3 (RMS Utilization Bound).** A set of $n$ independent periodic tasks with implicit deadlines is schedulable under RMS if:

$$U = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{C_i}{T_i} \leq n(2^{1/n} - 1)$$

As $n$ approaches infinity, this bound converges to $\ln(2) \approx 0.693$. This provides a sufficient but not necessary condition for schedulability. [Innovation](https://innovation.world/invention/rate-monotonic-scheduling/)

*Proof Sketch.* The bound is derived by finding the worst-case task set: a set of $n$ tasks that is just barely schedulable (any increase in $C_i$ causes a deadline miss) and achieves the minimum possible total utilisation. Liu and Layland showed this worst case arises when tasks have harmonically related periods with a specific load distribution. For $n=1$: $U \leq 1$, trivially. For $n=2$: the bound $2(2^{1/2}-1) \approx 0.828$ is tight for $T_2 = 2T_1/(2T_1 - T_1) \cdot T_1$ with appropriate WCET assignments. The general formula follows by induction on $n$ over this construction. $\square$

### 6.4 Exact Worst-Case Response Time Analysis

The utilization bound is sufficient but not necessary. A more powerful test is the exact Worst-Case Response Time (WCRT) analysis (Joseph and Pandya, 1986; Audsley et al., 1993).

**Definition 6.3 (Response Time).** The worst-case response time $R_i$ of task $\tau_i$ under RMS is the maximum time from release to completion over all possible phasing of higher-priority tasks.

By the Critical Instant Theorem (Theorem 6.1), the worst-case occurs at simultaneous release of all higher-priority tasks. Let $hp(i)$ denote the index set of all tasks with higher priority than $\tau_i$. At the critical instant, the total workload imposed on the processor in the interval $[0, R_i]$ satisfies:

$$R_i = C_i + \sum_{j \in hp(i)} \left\lceil \frac{R_i}{T_j} \right\rceil C_j$$

This is a fixed-point equation. It is solved iteratively via:

$$R_i^{(0)} = C_i, \qquad R_i^{(s+1)} = C_i + \sum_{j \in hp(i)} \left\lceil \frac{R_i^{(s)}}{T_j} \right\rceil C_j$$

**Theorem 6.4 (Convergence of WCRT Iteration).** The sequence $\{R_i^{(s)}\}$ is non-decreasing and converges to the least fixed point of the equation $R_i = C_i + \sum_{j \in hp(i)} \lceil R_i/T_j \rceil C_j$, or diverges beyond $T_i$ if the task set is unschedulable.

*Proof.* We show monotonicity: $R_i^{(1)} = C_i + \sum_{j} \lceil C_i / T_j \rceil C_j \geq C_i = R_i^{(0)}$. By induction, if $R_i^{(s)} \geq R_i^{(s-1)}$, then since $\lceil x/T_j \rceil$ is non-decreasing in $x$, we have $R_i^{(s+1)} \geq R_i^{(s)}$. The function $f(x) = C_i + \sum_j \lceil x/T_j \rceil C_j$ is a step function that increases by $C_j$ at every integer multiple of $T_j$. It is bounded from below by $C_i > 0$. The sequence is therefore non-decreasing and, if it remains bounded by $T_i$, converges to the least fixed point by the monotone convergence theorem. If $R_i^{(s)}$ exceeds $T_i$ for some $s$, the task will miss its deadline and the set is unschedulable. $\square$

**Corollary 6.1.** Task $\tau_i$ meets its deadline if and only if the iteration converges to $R_i^* \leq D_i$.

### 6.5 NeuroFabric Reflex Layer: Full Schedulability Analysis

We apply both tests to the NeuroFabric OS reflex layer $L_2$.

**Task set for $L_2$:**

