## Chapter 7 — The Whole-Body Control Layer: Full Mathematical Treatment

### 7.1 Equations of Motion

The dynamics of a humanoid robot with $n = 6 + m$ degrees of freedom under the floating-base convention are governed by the Equations of Motion (EOM):

$$M(q)\ddot{q} + C(q, \dot{q})\dot{q} + g(q) = S^T \tau + J_c^T(q) \lambda$$

where $M(q) \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ is the configuration-dependent mass-inertia matrix (symmetric positive definite); $C(q, \dot{q}) \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ is the Coriolis and centrifugal matrix (derivable from $M$ via the Christoffel symbol identity $C_{ij} = \frac{1}{2}\sum_k (\partial M_{ij}/\partial q_k + \partial M_{ik}/\partial q_j - \partial M_{jk}/\partial q_i)\dot{q}_k$); $g(q) = \partial V(q)/\partial q$ where $V$ is the gravitational potential energy; $S \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n}$ is the actuator selection matrix ($S = [0_{m \times 6}\ |\ I_m]$ under floating-base convention); $\tau \in \mathbb{R}^m$ is the joint torque vector; $J_c(q) \in \mathbb{R}^{3k \times n}$ is the stacked contact Jacobian for $k$ contact points; and $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}^{3k}$ is the contact force vector in world coordinates.

**Property 7.1 (Skew-Symmetry).** The matrix $\dot{M}(q) - 2C(q,\dot{q})$ is skew-symmetric. This property ensures energy consistency: multiplying the EOM on the left by $\dot{q}^T$ yields $\dot{q}^T M \ddot{q} + \dot{q}^T C \dot{q} = \frac{d}{dt}(\frac{1}{2}\dot{q}^T M \dot{q}) = \dot{q}^T \tau_{ext}$, confirming that the kinetic energy changes equal the power of external forces.

### 7.2 The Centroidal Momentum Matrix

The centroidal dynamics provide an efficient reduced-order model for balance control. Define the *Centroidal Momentum Matrix* (CMM) $A_G(q) \in \mathbb{R}^{6 \times n}$ such that:

$$h_G = A_G(q) \dot{q}$$

where $h_G = [l_G^T, k_G^T]^T \in \mathbb{R}^6$ is the centroidal momentum (linear $l_G \in \mathbb{R}^3$, angular $k_G \in \mathbb{R}^3$) expressed at the Centre of Mass (CoM). The CMM projects the generalised velocities of a humanoid robot to its spatial centroidal momentum, and its relationship to the joint-space inertia matrix can be shown through a transformation diagram. [ResearchGate](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/257523149_Centroidal_dynamics_of_a_humanoid_robot)

The time derivative yields the centroidal momentum rate equation:

$$\dot{h}_G = A_G(q)\ddot{q} + \dot{A}_G(q)\dot{q}$$

and from Newton-Euler principles applied at the CoM:

$$\dot{h}_G = \sum_{i=1}^{k} \begin{bmatrix} f_i \\ r_i \times f_i + \tau_i^{contact} \end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix} m_{\text{tot}}g \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}$$

where $f_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$ is the ground reaction force at contact $i$, $r_i = p_i - p_{CoM}$ is the moment arm from CoM to contact point $i$, $\tau_i^{contact}$ is the contact torque (zero for point contacts), $m_{\text{tot}}$ is total robot mass, and $g = [0, 0, -9.81]^T$ m/s² is the gravitational acceleration vector.

**Efficient Computation.** The CMM can be computed in $\mathcal{O}(n)$ operations using a spatial algebra recursive algorithm (Featherstone, 2008). This is critical for real-time control: at 500 Hz with $n=36$ DOF, the CMM must be recomputed in under 2 ms.

### 7.3 The WBC Quadratic Program

The Whole-Body Controller at each control step $t$ solves a weighted Quadratic Program (WQP) over decision variables $\mathbf{x} = (\ddot{q}, \tau, \lambda) \in \mathbb{R}^{n + m + 3k}$:

**Objective:**
$$\min_{\ddot{q}, \tau, \lambda} \sum_{j=1}^{N_{tasks}} w_j \|J_j(q) \ddot{q} + \dot{J}_j(q)\dot{q} - \ddot{x}_j^{des}\|_2^2 + w_\tau \|\tau\|_2^2 + w_\lambda \|\lambda\|_2^2$$

**Subject to:**
$$M(q)\ddot{q} + C(q,\dot{q})\dot{q} + g(q) = S^T \tau + J_c^T(q)\lambda \quad \text{(EOM)}$$
$$J_c(q)\ddot{q} + \dot{J}_c(q)\dot{q} = 0 \quad \text{(no-slip contact)}$$
$$\tau_{\min} \leq \tau \leq \tau_{\max} \quad \text{(torque limits)}$$
$$\lambda_{n,i} \geq 0 \quad \forall i \quad \text{(unilateral contact)}$$
$$\|\lambda_{t,i}\|_2 \leq \mu \lambda_{n,i} \quad \forall i \quad \text{(friction cone)}$$

where $J_j$ is the task Jacobian for task $j$, $\ddot{x}_j^{des}$ is the desired task-space acceleration for task $j$ (typically a PD feedback law $\ddot{x}_j^{des} = \ddot{x}_j^{ref} + K_d(\dot{x}_j^{ref} - \dot{x}_j) + K_p(x_j^{ref} - x_j)$), $w_j$ are task weights, $\lambda_{n,i}$ is the normal contact force at point $i$, $\lambda_{t,i}$ is the tangential force, and $\mu$ is the friction coefficient.

The friction cone constraint $\|\lambda_{t,i}\|_2 \leq \mu \lambda_{n,i}$ is a Second-Order Cone (SOC) constraint. For computational efficiency, it is linearized into a polyhedral cone with $N_{cone}$ facets:

$$\lambda_{t,i} \cdot d_{\ell} \leq \frac{\mu}{\cos(\pi/N_{cone})} \lambda_{n,i}, \quad \ell = 1, \ldots, N_{cone}$$

where $d_\ell$ are unit vectors in the tangential plane at equally spaced angles. This linearization converts the problem to a pure QP solvable with active-set or interior-point methods.

### 7.4 KKT Optimality Conditions

We rewrite the WQP in standard form. Denote $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_x}$ as the concatenated decision variable, $H \succeq 0$ as the Hessian of the quadratic cost, $c$ as the linear cost vector, $A_{eq}$ and $b_{eq}$ as equality constraint matrices (encoding EOM and contact constraints), $A_{ineq}$ and $b_{ineq}$ as inequality constraint matrices (torque limits and friction cone):

$$\min_{\mathbf{x}} \frac{1}{2} \mathbf{x}^T H \mathbf{x} + c^T \mathbf{x} \quad \text{s.t.} \quad A_{eq}\mathbf{x} = b_{eq}, \quad A_{ineq}\mathbf{x} \leq b_{ineq}$$

The Lagrangian is:

$$\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x}, \mu_{eq}, \mu_{ineq}) = \frac{1}{2}\mathbf{x}^T H \mathbf{x} + c^T \mathbf{x} + \mu_{eq}^T(A_{eq}\mathbf{x} - b_{eq}) + \mu_{ineq}^T(A_{ineq}\mathbf{x} - b_{ineq})$$

**KKT Conditions (Necessary and Sufficient for Convex QP):** A primal-dual pair $(\mathbf{x}^*, \mu_{eq}^*, \mu_{ineq}^*)$ is optimal if and only if:

**(1) Stationarity:**
$$H\mathbf{x}^* + c + A_{eq}^T \mu_{eq}^* + A_{ineq}^T \mu_{ineq}^* = 0$$

**(2) Primal feasibility:**
$$A_{eq}\mathbf{x}^* = b_{eq}, \qquad A_{ineq}\mathbf{x}^* \leq b_{ineq}$$

**(3) Dual feasibility:**
$$\mu_{ineq}^* \geq 0$$

**(4) Complementary slackness:**
$$\mu_{ineq,i}^* \cdot (A_{ineq}\mathbf{x}^* - b_{ineq})_i = 0, \quad \forall i$$

**Theorem 7.1 (Strong Duality).** The WBC QP satisfies strong duality: the duality gap at optimality is zero.

*Proof.* The objective $\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{x}^T H \mathbf{x} + c^T\mathbf{x}$ is convex (since $H \succeq 0$). We verify Slater's constraint qualification: there exists a strictly interior feasible point. The equality constraints (EOM, contact no-slip) are linear and their feasible set is non-empty for any physically realised robot configuration (the robot exists). The inequality constraints (torque bounds) are satisfiable at $\tau = 0$ for moderate accelerations. The friction cone is satisfiable for $\lambda = 0$ (robot at rest with no contact). Under these conditions, Slater's condition holds, and by the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker theorem for convex programs, strong duality is guaranteed. $\square$

**Implication for Real-Time Solving.** Since $H \succeq 0$ and Slater's condition holds, the QP has a unique primal solution (assuming $H \succ 0$, which follows from the regularisation terms $w_\tau, w_\lambda > 0$) and the KKT system can be solved to arbitrary precision. An active-set method exploiting temporal warm-starting (the active set rarely changes between consecutive control steps at 500 Hz) achieves near-linear complexity in practice. By exploiting sparsity and temporal structure in the optimization with a custom active-set algorithm, control rates of 1 kHz for a 34-DOF humanoid are achievable. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1311.1839)

### 7.5 The MPC Layer: Finite-Horizon Centroidal Optimal Control

The MPC layer solves, at frequency $f_{MPC} \approx 50$ Hz, a finite-horizon optimal control problem over the centroidal state:

$$\min_{\{x_k, u_k\}} \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} \left[ \|x_k - x_k^{ref}\|_{Q}^2 + \|u_k\|_{R}^2 \right] + \|x_N - x_N^{ref}\|_{Q_f}^2$$

subject to:
$$x_{k+1} = A_d x_k + B_d u_k + g_d \quad \text{(linearised centroidal dynamics)}$$
$$x_k \in \mathcal{X}, \quad u_k \in \mathcal{U} \quad \forall k$$
$$x_0 = \hat{x}_0 \quad \text{(current state estimate)}$$

where $x_k = (p_{CoM,k},\ v_{CoM,k},\ k_{G,k}) \in \mathbb{R}^9$ is the centroidal state (CoM position, velocity, angular momentum), $u_k = (f_{1,k}, \ldots, f_{c,k}) \in \mathbb{R}^{3c}$ are the contact forces, and $(A_d, B_d, g_d)$ are obtained by Euler discretisation of the centroidal dynamics at step size $\Delta t = 1/f_{MPC}$:

$$A_d = I + \Delta t \cdot A_c, \quad B_d = \Delta t \cdot B_c(r_{1,k},\ldots,r_{c,k})$$

where $A_c$ is the continuous-time system matrix and $B_c$ encodes the moment arms $r_i = p_{contact,i} - p_{CoM}$ (updated each control step). The constraint sets $\mathcal{X}$ (CoM height bounds, angular momentum bounds) and $\mathcal{U}$ (friction cone per contact) are both polytopes, making the MPC a linearly-constrained QP solvable in $\mathcal{O}(N^3 n_x^3)$ via sequential QP or $\mathcal{O}(N n_x^2)$ via Riccati recursion for the unconstrained case.

