## Chapter 13 — Discussion and Limitations

### 13.1 Comparison with Existing Systems

**Versus ROS 2.** NeuroFabric OS provides formal real-time guarantees (Theorem 6.4), GPU-native scheduling with CUDA Graph integration (Section 11.3), and the Neural Module first-class abstraction, none of which are present in ROS 2. The cost is significantly higher implementation complexity and a steeper learning curve. ROS 2 remains appropriate for research systems where flexibility and ecosystem breadth outweigh timing guarantees.

**Versus NVIDIA Isaac ROS.** Isaac ROS provides GPU acceleration and simulation integration but does not offer a formal scheduling theory, the Neural Module lifecycle system, or the DEM hierarchy. NeuroFabric OS can be understood as providing the formal theoretical underpinning for what Isaac ROS approaches pragmatically.

**Versus Game Engines (Unreal Engine Chaos Physics, Unity).** Game engines provide outstanding simulation fidelity and asset pipelines but provide no formal real-time control guarantees, no WBC integration, and no safety architecture.

### 13.2 Open Research Problems

**Problem 1 — Probabilistic WCET for Neural Inference.** Current WCET analysis tools, such as static analysis on control-flow graphs and hybrid measurement-based methods, assume deterministic code paths. Neural network inference time on GPU is non-deterministic due to warp divergence, cache effects, and kernel autotuning. Extending WCET theory to probabilistic execution time distributions (pWCET) for GPU-resident neural networks remains an important open problem. Estimating the WCET of GPU-accelerated applications using hybrid analysis and time-predictable warp scheduling represent current research directions for GPU real-time systems. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0141933125000705)

**Problem 2 — Global Convergence of InEKF under Large Initial Error.** The local convergence proofs for the InEKF are based on Lipschitz bounds of the nonlinear terms, and if the state estimate is initialised poorly, it is possible for the filter to diverge. [Sage Journals](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0278364919894385) Global convergence guarantees for the InEKF on $SE_2(3)$ under large initial orientation error remain an open question.

**Problem 3 — Memory Consolidation at Operational Scale.** The episodic-to-semantic consolidation pipeline requires extracting structured knowledge from continuous sensorimotor experience. Current sparse autoencoder and LLM-based extraction methods are not yet computationally feasible at the rate required for a continuously operating robot (approximately one consolidation per minute of operation over a year-long deployment).

**Problem 4 — Sim-to-Real Gap for Full HTCG.** While individual modules can be trained and validated in simulation, the full HTCG introduces emergent timing behaviours under real-world noise, contact transitions, and compute load that are not faithfully reproduced in simulation. Closing this gap requires formal sim-to-real transfer theory for graph-orchestrated systems.

**Problem 5 — Formal Verification of Neural Policies.** Neural Module sandboxing bounds worst-case computation time and monitors output statistics, but does not formally verify policy correctness properties (e.g., "the balance policy never commands a joint torque that violates the friction cone"). Integrating neural network formal verification tools (e.g., $\alpha$-$\beta$-CROWN, Marabou) into the module lifecycle is an important safety-critical future direction.

### 13.3 Limitations of the Present Work

This dissertation is theoretical. The WBC QP solve times and reflex WCET values are representative estimates from the literature, not measured on a specific physical platform. The DEM retention policy parameters ($\lambda$, $\epsilon_{forget}$) require empirical calibration for each deployment scenario. The InEKF derivation assumes smooth contact transitions; impact dynamics introduce impulse forces not modelled by the continuous-time system model. The Cognitive Fault Isolation Theorem (Theorem 12.1) guarantees stability only for initial states in $\mathcal{X}_{stable}$; the size and shape of this set must be characterised for each specific robot platform by Lyapunov analysis or sampling-based verification.

