## Chapter 14 — Conclusion

This dissertation has presented **NeuroFabric OS**, a formally specified AI-native operating system architecture for general-purpose humanoid robots. The core thesis — that humanoid robots require an AI-native, GPU-primary, graph-orchestrated operating system treating neural policies as first-class system objects — is supported by the formal architecture developed herein and by convergent trends in the robotics and AI literature.

The principal contributions are:

**1.** The *cyber-physical organism* model $\mathcal{H}$ and the multi-timescale coherence problem (Definition 3.1) providing a rigorous motivation for the architectural requirements.

**2.** The *Hierarchical Temporal Compute Graph* (HTCG) with a complete structural-induction proof of the end-to-end latency bound (Theorem 5.1).

**3.** A complete real-time schedulability analysis for the reflex layer including: the Critical Instant Theorem (Theorem 6.1), RMS Optimality (Theorem 6.2), the Utilization Bound (Theorem 6.3), exact Worst-Case Response Time iteration (Theorem 6.4), and a tight NP-hardness reduction for the general HTCG scheduling problem via 3-PARTITION (Theorem 6.5).

**4.** A full mathematical treatment of the WBC QP including EOM derivation, centroidal dynamics, KKT optimality conditions (Section 7.4), and strong duality under Slater's qualification (Theorem 7.1).

**5.** A rigorous derivation of the DDPM diffusion policy from the variational ELBO including the complete noise schedule mathematics, noise-prediction parameterisation, and receding-horizon integration (Section 8.3).

**6.** The *Distributed Embodied Memory* hierarchy with formal stochastic retention policy and episodic-to-semantic consolidation operator (Chapter 9).

**7.** An Invariant Extended Kalman Filter formulation on $SE_2(3)$ for digital twin synchronisation with formal Mahalanobis divergence detection (Chapter 10).

**8.** The *Cognitive Fault Isolation Theorem* (Theorem 12.1) guaranteeing postural stability and collision avoidance under simultaneous failure of all layers $L_3$–$L_5$.

As many models suitable for handling different tasks on humanoid robots — visual understanding, motion planning, control — are currently isolated and have not yet been successfully integrated, a modular and hierarchical approach to handle this complexity is necessary. NeuroFabric OS provides a principled theoretical foundation for that integration, grounding it in formal proofs, classical real-time systems theory, convex optimisation, Lie group geometry, and probabilistic generative modelling.

