Appendix A: Proofs for the Hierarchical Temporal Compute Graph (HTCG)

This appendix provides complete proofs for all formal properties of the Hierarchical Temporal Compute Graph introduced in Chapter 5.

Definition A.1 (HTCG, restated).
The Hierarchical Temporal Compute Graph is a tuple \mathcal{G}=(V,E,W,\mathcal{H},\Phi) where
V is the set of compute nodes, each v = (f_v,\mathcal{X}_v^{in},\mathcal{X}_v^{out},T_v,\mathcal{D}_v,\pi_v,\kappa_v),
E\subseteq V\times V is the set of directed data‑dependency edges,
W:E\to\mathbb{R}_{>0} assigns worst‑case transmission latency to each edge,
\mathcal{H}:V\to\{1,\dots,5\} assigns a layer, and
\Phi:E\to\{\mathrm{sync},\mathrm{async}\} classifies edges.
The subgraph induced by synchronous edges, \mathcal{G}|_{\mathrm{sync}}=(V,E_{\mathrm{sync}}) with E_{\mathrm{sync}}=\{e\in E:\Phi(e)=\mathrm{sync}\}, is required to be a directed acyclic graph (DAG).

Lemma A.1 (DAG topological order).
Because \mathcal{G}|_{\mathrm{sync}} is a DAG, there exists a topological ordering v_1\prec v_2\prec\cdots\prec v_{|V|} such that for every synchronous edge (v_i,v_j) we have i<j.

Proof. A DAG always admits a topological ordering by standard graph theory (e.g., Kahn’s algorithm or DFS). Existence is immediate from the definition. \square

