A.1 End‑to‑End Latency Bound

Theorem 5.1 (HTCG Path Latency Bound).
For any directed path P=(v_1,\dots,v_k) in \mathcal{G}|_{\mathrm{sync}}, the end‑to‑end latency satisfies

\mathcal{L}(P) \le \sum_{i=1}^{k} \mathrm{WCET}(v_i) + \sum_{i=1}^{k-1} W(v_i,v_{i+1}).

Proof (by strong induction on path length k).

Base case k=1. A single‑node path has latency equal to the time from input arrival to output emission, which by definition of worst‑case execution time is bounded by \mathrm{WCET}(v_1). The sum over empty edge set is zero, so \mathcal{L}(P)=\mathrm{WCET}(v_1)\le\mathrm{WCET}(v_1). ✓

Inductive step. Assume the bound holds for all paths of length k-1. Consider a path P=(v_1,\dots,v_k). Node v_k can start computing only after it has received its required input from v_{k-1}. The earliest time node v_{k-1} finishes and emits its output is bounded by the end‑to‑end latency of the prefix path \mathcal{L}(v_1,\dots,v_{k-1}). The transmission along edge (v_{k-1},v_k) incurs at most W(v_{k-1},v_k) additional delay (by definition of W). Finally, node v_k requires at most \mathrm{WCET}(v_k) to produce its own output. Hence

\mathcal{L}(P) \le \mathcal{L}(v_1,\dots,v_{k-1}) + W(v_{k-1},v_k) + \mathrm{WCET}(v_k).

Applying the induction hypothesis to the prefix yields

\mathcal{L}(P) \le \Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{k-1} \mathrm{WCET}(v_i) + \sum_{i=1}^{k-2} W(v_i,v_{i+1})\Bigr) + W(v_{k-1},v_k) + \mathrm{WCET}(v_k),

which simplifies to the desired formula. \square

Corollary A.1 (Critical reflex path budget).
For the three‑node path P_{\mathrm{reflex}} = (\text{IMU}, \text{StateEstimator}, \text{TorqueLoop}) with a 1 ms overall deadline, the sum of WCETs and transmission delays must satisfy

\mathrm{WCET(IMU)} + W(\text{IMU,SE}) + \mathrm{WCET(SE)} + W(\text{SE,TL}) + \mathrm{WCET(TL)} \le 1\;\mathrm{ms}.

Proof. Direct substitution of Theorem 5.1 with the hard real‑time requirement \mathcal{L}(P_{\mathrm{reflex}})\le 1\;\mathrm{ms}. \square

