A.2 Temporal Consistency Guarantee

Definition 5.4 (Temporal Consistency).
An execution trace \sigma is temporally consistent if for every synchronous edge (u,v)\in E_{\mathrm{sync}},

t_{\mathrm{receive}}(v,\sigma) - t_{\mathrm{emit}}(u,\sigma) \le W(u,v) + \epsilon_{\mathrm{sys}},

where \epsilon_{\mathrm{sys}}\ge 0 is a system‑wide jitter tolerance.

Proposition A.1 (Schedulability implies temporal consistency).
If every node v meets its deadline \mathcal{D}_v under the HTCG dispatcher and the transmission mechanism respects the worst‑case delays W, then every synchronous edge satisfies temporal consistency.

Proof. Let (u,v)\in E_{\mathrm{sync}} and consider any execution where both jobs are released according to their periodic timelines. Because \mathcal{G}|_{\mathrm{sync}} is a DAG, data flow is unidirectional; thus v consumes the output produced by u in the same graph iteration. The dispatcher ensures that v does not start before its input is available. By the deadline guarantee, u completes by its absolute deadline, which is at most T_u after release. The buffering and transport mechanism (lock‑free ring buffer with bounded latency) delivers the tensor to v within W(u,v) time units after u’s emission. Consequently, the receive time at v is bounded by t_{\mathrm{emit}}(u) + W(u,v) + \text{(scheduling jitter)}. Choosing \epsilon_{\mathrm{sys}} as the maximum scheduler‑induced jitter yields the bound. \square

