Appendix C: Whole‑Body Control QP – Complete Optimality Proofs

C.1 Equations of Motion and Energy Consistency

Property 7.1 (Skew‑symmetry).
\dot{M}(q) - 2C(q,\dot{q}) is skew‑symmetric, i.e., x^T(\dot{M}-2C)x = 0 for any vector x\in\mathbb{R}^n.

Proof.
The Coriolis matrix can be derived from M(q) using the Christoffel symbols:

C_{ij} = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^{n} \Bigl( \frac{\partial M_{ij}}{\partial q_k} + \frac{\partial M_{ik}}{\partial q_j} - \frac{\partial M_{jk}}{\partial q_i} \Bigr) \dot{q}_k .

Differentiating M(q) with respect to time gives \dot{M}_{ij} = \sum_k \frac{\partial M_{ij}}{\partial q_k} \dot{q}_k. For any vector x, compute

x^T(\dot{M} - 2C)x = \sum_{i,j} x_i \Bigl( \sum_k \frac{\partial M_{ij}}{\partial q_k}\dot{q}_k \Bigr) x_j - \sum_{i,j} x_i \Bigl( \sum_k \bigl( \frac{\partial M_{ij}}{\partial q_k} + \frac{\partial M_{ik}}{\partial q_j} - \frac{\partial M_{jk}}{\partial q_i} \bigr) \dot{q}_k \Bigr) x_j .

The terms \sum_k \frac{\partial M_{ij}}{\partial q_k}\dot{q}_k cancel, leaving

-\sum_{i,j} x_i \Bigl( \sum_k \frac{\partial M_{ik}}{\partial q_j} \dot{q}_k \Bigr) x_j + \sum_{i,j} x_i \Bigl( \sum_k \frac{\partial M_{jk}}{\partial q_i} \dot{q}_k \Bigr) x_j .

Renaming indices shows these two sums are negatives of each other, hence the total is zero. \square

C.2 Convexity of the QP and Slater’s Condition

Theorem C.1 (WBC QP is convex). The weighted least‑squares objective with quadratic regularisation terms, together with linear equality and convex inequality constraints, forms a convex quadratic program.

Proof. The objective can be written as \frac{1}{2} \mathbf{x}^T H \mathbf{x} + c^T \mathbf{x} where the Hessian H is a block‑diagonal matrix assembled from the task Jacobians J_j and the regularisation weights w_\tau, w_\lambda. Each block J_j^T w_j J_j \succeq 0 and the regularisation adds positive semi‑definiteness; together with w_\tau I \succ 0 and w_\lambda I \succ 0, the overall Hessian is positive definite (H\succ 0). All constraints (equations of motion, contact no‑slip, torque bounds, linearised friction cone) are linear equalities and inequalities, hence define a convex feasible set. A convex objective over a convex set yields a convex optimisation problem. \square

Theorem 7.1 (Strong duality). The WBC QP satisfies strong duality; the duality gap at optimality is zero.

Proof. Slater’s condition requires a strictly feasible point. Because the robot is a physical system, there exists a configuration with zero joint torques (\tau = 0) and trivial contact forces (\lambda=0) that satisfies the equations of motion for \ddot{q}=0 (static equilibrium) if gravity is included; the equality constraints M(q)\ddot{q}+C\dot{q}+g = S^T\tau + J_c^T\lambda are linear and non‑empty. The torque limits are strict: \tau_{\min} < 0 < \tau_{\max}, so there is an interior point with \tau=0. The linearised friction cone \|\lambda_{t,i}\| \le \mu \lambda_{n,i} is satisfied for \lambda=0 (though the origin is on the boundary of the cone; however, a strictly interior point can be found by taking a small positive normal force and zero tangential force, which satisfies the strict inequality if \mu>0). Hence there exists \mathbf{x} strictly satisfying all inequality constraints. With convex objective and linear constraints, Slater’s condition implies strong duality. \square

C.3 KKT Conditions Derivation

Theorem C.2 (KKT conditions for the WBC QP).
Consider the convex QP

\min_{\mathbf{x}} \frac{1}{2}\mathbf{x}^T H \mathbf{x} + c^T \mathbf{x}
\quad\text{s.t.}\quad A_{\mathrm{eq}}\mathbf{x} = b_{\mathrm{eq}},\; A_{\mathrm{ineq}}\mathbf{x} \le b_{\mathrm{ineq}} .

A feasible point \mathbf{x}^* is globally optimal iff there exist multipliers \mu_{\mathrm{eq}},\mu_{\mathrm{ineq}} such that

\begin{aligned}
\text{(Stationarity)} &\quad H\mathbf{x}^* + c + A_{\mathrm{eq}}^T \mu_{\mathrm{eq}} + A_{\mathrm{ineq}}^T \mu_{\mathrm{ineq}} = 0,\\
\text{(Primal feasibility)} &\quad A_{\mathrm{eq}}\mathbf{x}^* = b_{\mathrm{eq}},\quad A_{\mathrm{ineq}}\mathbf{x}^* \le b_{\mathrm{ineq}},\\
\text{(Dual feasibility)} &\quad \mu_{\mathrm{ineq}} \ge 0,\\
\text{(Complementary slackness)} &\quad \mu_{\mathrm{ineq},i}\,(A_{\mathrm{ineq}}\mathbf{x}^* - b_{\mathrm{ineq}})_i = 0 \quad\forall i.
\end{aligned}

Proof. For convex problems where Slater’s condition holds, the KKT conditions are necessary and sufficient for global optimality. The Lagrangian is

\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x},\mu_{\mathrm{eq}},\mu_{\mathrm{ineq}}) = \frac{1}{2}\mathbf{x}^T H \mathbf{x} + c^T \mathbf{x} + \mu_{\mathrm{eq}}^T(A_{\mathrm{eq}}\mathbf{x}-b_{\mathrm{eq}}) + \mu_{\mathrm{ineq}}^T(A_{\mathrm{ineq}}\mathbf{x}-b_{\mathrm{ineq}}).

Setting \nabla_{\mathbf{x}} \mathcal{L}=0 yields stationarity; primal feasibility is the constraint satisfaction; dual feasibility on inequality multipliers ensures the correct sign for minimization; complementary slackness encodes that inactive constraints have zero multiplier. Because the problem is convex and Slater holds, any KKT point is a global minimiser and the dual optimum attains the same value (strong duality). \square

