Appendix E: Distributed Embodied Memory – Retention and Consolidation

E.1 Stochastic Retention Probability

Definition 9.2 (Retention probability).
For an episode e_i stored in episodic memory \mathcal{M}_3,

P_{\mathrm{retain}}(i,t) = e^{-\lambda (t - t_i) / S_i},

where \lambda>0 is the forgetting rate, t_i the timestamp, and S_i the salience defined as

S_i = w_r |r_i| + w_n \mathrm{Nov}(o_i) + w_s \mathrm{Sem}(i).

Derivation rationale. The exponential decay form follows the classical Ebbinghaus forgetting curve, modulated by salience. Higher salience slows forgetting (effectively scaling time). The salience components capture reward surprise (magnitude), novelty (1 minus maximal cosine similarity with other stored episodes), and semantic consolidation status (binary).

Lemma E.1 (Expected lifetime). The expected time until eviction (when P_{\mathrm{retain}} < \epsilon_{\mathrm{forget}}) is

t_{\mathrm{evict}} = t_i + \frac{S_i}{\lambda} \ln(1/\epsilon_{\mathrm{forget}}).

Proof. Solve e^{-\lambda (t-t_i)/S_i} = \epsilon_{\mathrm{forget}} → -(t-t_i)\lambda/S_i = \ln\epsilon_{\mathrm{forget}} → t-t_i = \frac{S_i}{\lambda}\ln(1/\epsilon_{\mathrm{forget}}). \square

E.2 Consolidation Operator Properties

Definition 9.3 (Consolidation operator \mathcal{C}). Given a batch \mathcal{B} of episodes, \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{B}) outputs a set of triples (e_1, rel, e_2, c) with confidence c. These are merged into the knowledge graph \mathcal{G}_{KG} with conflict resolution:

\text{If } \exists\, (e_1, rel, e_2^{\mathrm{old}}): \text{ replace with } e_2^{\mathrm{new}} \text{ if } c_{\mathrm{new}} > c_{\mathrm{old}}.

Proposition E.1 (Monotonic knowledge refinement). The merge operation ensures that for any entity pair, the stored edge confidence is non‑decreasing over time.

Proof. The rule only updates when the new confidence is strictly greater. Thus edge confidences form a monotonic sequence bounded by 1, guaranteeing convergence. \square

