Appendix G: GPU‑Native Compute Fabric – Formal Guarantees

G.1 CUDA Graph Capture and Determinism

Proposition G.1 (Deterministic execution). Once captured, a CUDA graph produces identical kernel launch sequences and memory operations for every invocation, provided the graph topology and kernel parameters remain unchanged. This guarantees that the worst‑case execution time (WCET) of the graph can be bounded by the maximum observed time over a series of profiling runs plus a safety margin.

Proof (sketch). CUDA graphs record the exact sequence of operations, eliminating CPU‑side launch overhead. The execution time on GPU is subject to hardware variability (cache, warp scheduling), but the maximum observed time across multiple runs on the same hardware gives an empirical WCET bound. Determinism is guaranteed by the CUDA runtime as long as no conditional kernels are used. \square

G.2 Zero‑Copy Tensor Table Consistency

Definition G.1 (Double‑buffer protocol). Each tensor slot in the shared table consists of two buffers B_0, B_1 and an atomic flag f (0/1). The writer always writes to the buffer indicated by f\oplus 1 (where \oplus is XOR), then atomically toggles f. The reader reads from the buffer indicated by the current value of f and checks that the flag has not changed during the read.

Proposition G.2 (Data consistency). Under this protocol, a reader always sees a fully written, consistent tensor snapshot, never a partially updated tensor.

Proof. The writer completes its write before toggling the flag. Since the flag is atomic, the reader sees either the old complete buffer (flag not toggled yet) or the new complete buffer (after toggle). Because the writer never writes to the buffer currently pointed to by the flag, no concurrent modification occurs. Thus the snapshot is consistent. \square

G.3 Module Eviction Optimality

Definition 11.1 (Eviction score). Under memory pressure, the score for module \mathcal{N} is

\mathrm{score}_{\mathrm{evict}} = \frac{t_{\mathrm{now}} - t_{\mathrm{last\_use}}(\mathcal{N})}{\pi(\mathcal{N}) \cdot \mathrm{size}(\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{GPU}}(\mathcal{N}))}.

Proposition G.3 (Greedy eviction is near‑optimal for linear penalty). If the penalty for evicting a module is proportional to (priority × last‑use recency) and storage cost is memory size, then selecting the module with the highest score approximates the optimal solution to the knapsack‑like eviction problem.

Proof. The greedy heuristic sorts by ratio (benefit of keeping)/(cost). Here, benefit is modelled as recency‑weighted priority, cost is memory size. This ratio rule is optimal for the fractional knapsack problem; for the integer case it provides a feasible approximation. \square

