Appendix H: Safety and Fault‑Tolerance – Complete Proof of Cognitive Fault Isolation

H.1 Theorem 12.1 (Cognitive Fault Isolation)

Theorem 12.1. Assume the NeuroFabric OS architecture satisfies the layer interface contracts (Definition 4.1) and the reflex layer schedulability conditions (exact WCRT analysis, Theorem 6.4). If all nodes in layers L_3, L_4, L_5 simultaneously fail (produce no output or invalid output), then the humanoid robot:
(a) maintains postural stability for all t > t_{\mathrm{fail}}, provided \|x(t_{\mathrm{fail}})\|\in\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{stable}};
(b) avoids self‑collision and environmental collision for t\in[t_{\mathrm{fail}}, t_{\mathrm{fail}}+T_{\mathrm{safe}}] where T_{\mathrm{safe}} is the duration of a pre‑loaded safe‑stop trajectory.

Proof.

(a) Postural stability.
By Definition 4.1, the downward interface from L_3 to L_2 includes a fallback behaviour \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{fallback}} activated when the contractual deadline \Delta t_{\mathrm{contract}} is missed. In the case of L_3 failure, this fallback is a zero‑velocity reference trajectory (static balance).
The layer L_2 (Deterministic Control) continues to execute the Whole‑Body Control QP at its prescribed rate (500 Hz). Theorem 6.4 guarantees that all L_2 tasks meet their deadlines. Therefore the WBC QP is solved on schedule.
Under the static balance objective, the WBC minimises CoM acceleration and attempts to keep the Zero‑Moment Point inside the support polygon. The QP objective is strictly convex (Theorem C.1) and its KKT point is the unique global minimiser. The resulting feedback control law is asymptotically stable for the linearised centroidal dynamics within a neighbourhood \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{stable}} of the upright posture. Since the initial state is assumed to lie in this region, postural stability is maintained indefinitely.

(b) Collision avoidance.
The CollisionReflex task \tau_4 runs entirely within L_2 with worst‑case response time R_4^* = 0.48\;\mathrm{ms} (verified in Section 6.5). It continuously monitors joint positions against a self‑collision model stored in L_2 ROM. If the distance to a collision boundary falls below a safe threshold, it injects a repulsive joint torque via the hardware abstraction layer. This mechanism requires no input from upper layers and remains operational.

For environmental collision, a safe‑stop trajectory \pi_{\mathrm{safe}} is pre‑loaded into L_2 memory at boot time. Upon detection of L_3–L_5 failure (via watchdog or contract violation), L_2 immediately initiates \pi_{\mathrm{safe}}. Because the trajectory is known to be collision‑free by offline verification and is executed within T_{\mathrm{safe}} (e.g., 2 s), the robot avoids environmental collisions during the stop. The safe‑stop is itself a valid trajectory for the WBC, ensuring that all physical constraints remain satisfied. \square

---

H.2 Degradation Level Transitions

Proposition H.1 (Safe transition). The degradation level transitions defined in Section 12.2 are monotonic in the set of failed layers, and each transition preserves the safety guarantees of the surviving lower layers.

Proof. Each degradation level disables higher layers while leaving lower layers fully operational. Because lower layers are designed with fallbacks that do not depend on upper layers (Definition 4.1) and are schedulable under all failure scenarios (since worst‑case interference is bounded), the guarantees propagate downward. \square

