Appendix I: Additional Formal Architecture Proofs

I.1 Ring Buffer Non‑Blocking Property

Proposition I.1 (Ring buffer capacity ensures no overwrite). Consider a single‑producer single‑consumer lock‑free ring buffer of size B, where the producer (layer L_i) writes at period T_i and the consumer (L_{i-1}) reads at period T_{i-1}\le T_i. If the contractual deadline \Delta t_{\mathrm{contract}} is respected, choosing

B \ge \left\lceil \frac{\Delta t_{\mathrm{contract}}}{T_i} \right\rceil + 1

guarantees that a new message never overwrites an unread message.

Proof. The producer inserts at most \lceil \Delta t_{\mathrm{contract}}/T_i \rceil messages before the consumer is contractually obligated to have read at least one (due to the deadline). Adding one extra slot ensures the buffer never wraps around onto an unread entry. \square

I.2 Neural Module Hot‑Reload Safety

Property 8.1 (Output distribution continuity). The hot‑reload is permitted only if

\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{val}}}\bigl[\|f_\theta(x) - f_{\theta'}(x)\|_2\bigr] \le \delta_{\mathrm{reload}}.

Proposition I.2 (No unsafe output during swap). Because the atomic pointer swap occurs between two consecutive inference calls, the actuator never sees a mixture of old and new parameters. The output of the new module is a single consistent snapshot.

Proof. Double‑buffering ensures that either the old parameters \theta or the new \theta' are used for the entire forward pass. Atomic swap on a GPU pointer (using CUDA atomic operations on a unified memory pointer) completes between kernel launches. Therefore the decision to swap is isolated from the inference pipeline. \square


